---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const chapters = [
  {
    title: 'Error detection',
    blurb:
      'How a receiver decides whether the bits that arrived are the bits that were sent — and, more interestingly, when it gets that decision wrong.',
    links: [
      { href: '/crc', name: 'CRC', desc: 'Polynomial division over GF(2). Long-division table, generation and verification for any generator.' },
      { href: '/circuit', name: 'Circuit', desc: 'The shift register that computes a CRC in hardware, drawn for whatever polynomial you give it, with a clock stepper.' },
      { href: '/matrix', name: 'Generator matrix', desc: 'Deriving G and H for the cyclic code behind a CRC, one row at a time, with the G·Hᵀ = 0 check.' },
      { href: '/vrc-lrc', name: 'VRC + LRC', desc: 'Two-dimensional parity, and the four-bit rectangle that slips straight past it.' },
      { href: '/checksum', name: 'Checksum', desc: "RFC 1071 one's-complement sum and plain modular sum, plus the compensating errors they cannot see." },
      { href: '/lab', name: 'Error lab', desc: 'One frame, four schemes, arbitrary corruption. Watch which ones catch it and which do not.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Addressing',
    blurb: 'Splitting an IPv4 block to fit a given number of hosts or subnets.',
    links: [
      { href: '/subnetting', name: 'Subnetting', desc: 'Calculator, equal-size splits, and VLSM — with the general sizing rule stated rather than worked for one example.' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Error Detection & Subnetting"
  description="Interactive CRC, VRC, LRC, checksum and IPv4 subnetting calculators with step-by-step derivations."
>
  <h1>Error detection &amp; subnetting</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Interactive tools for two chapters of a computer-networks course. Every calculator shows its
    working, and the error lab exists to demonstrate the part textbooks state but rarely show: these
    schemes have specific, predictable blind spots.
  </p>

  {chapters.map((chapter) => (
    <section>
      <h2>{chapter.title}</h2>
      <p class="lede">{chapter.blurb}</p>
      <div class="home-grid">
        {chapter.links.map((link) => (
          <a class="home-card" href={link.href}>
            <h3>{link.name}</h3>
            <p>{link.desc}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>How the pieces connect</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      The CRC page, the circuit page and the generator-matrix page are three views of a single
      computation — XOR long division of the message by g(x):
    </p>
    <ul class="steps">
      <li>
        The <strong>long-division table</strong> shows the generator XORed in at each alignment, with
        the remainder settling into the r bits the appended zeros freed up.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <strong>shift register</strong> does the same arithmetic one clock at a time. Its
        contents after each clock are the same numbers as the working register in the table.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <strong>generator matrix</strong> collects the result of dividing each x<sup>n−1−i</sup>
        by g(x); those remainders are the parity columns of G, and encoding with it reproduces
        message + FCS exactly.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">
      Subnetting is unrelated to any of that — it shares only the page furniture and a fondness for
      counting bits.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>A note on which CRC this is</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p style="margin:0">
      CRCs here use the textbook form: no initial value, no bit reflection, no final XOR. That keeps
      the arithmetic identical to the division table and the circuit shown next to it. Named
      real-world variants (CRC-32 as used by Ethernet and ZIP, for instance) layer those extra
      parameters on top, so their published check values will not match what this site prints for
      the same polynomial. The polynomial, the division, and the guarantees are the same.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
